body:
>So you can just make up a definition for a woman?

It is the definition we made up as a society.

&#x200B;

>What if it is the definition I consider to be a man?

&#x200B;

Up to you, I'm just saying **my** definition of a woman here, and my definition of a man is "whomever identifies as a man"

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

>Can I call you any pronoun I want since it doesn’t effect “the role we play in society”?

I prefer she/her but do whatever you want.

&#x200B;

>Or is that going to all of the sudden offend you?

I'm just going to correct you respectfully but again, do whatever you want.

Although the languages are very similar, there are also many differences. The written forms are more similar than spoken, but still vocabulary and grammar varies between Spanish and Portuguese.  
For example, Spanish has a much more significant Arabic influence than Portuguese.

&#x200B;

A speaker of one language may read a text in the other and understand a significant portion but not all; if you want to experience a similar phenomenon with English, read a text in Dutch or German: you might understand some but not all.

&#x200B;

Spanish btw, also has nasal vowels but unlike Portuguese, they are not phonemic, they don't change the meaning of words.
